{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q005_cupredoxin_function_transfer","answer":"SCOPe places these domains in different families within the Cupredoxins superfamily (b.6.1), in the cupredoxin-like fold (b.6) of the all-beta class (b). This classification concerns individual domains, not necessarily the architecture of the whole protein. Their shared superfamily indicates inferred remote common ancestry despite substantial functional divergence; sharing only a fold would establish structural resemblance without necessarily establishing homology. They are not all members of the plastocyanin/azurin-like family.

Copper binding and electron transfer should not be transferred across this superfamily. Plastocyanin and azurin are established type-1 copper electron carriers. Ephrin ectodomains instead bind Eph receptors and participate in cell-surface signaling; their cupredoxin ancestry does not make them copper-dependent electron carriers. PAD4 is a calcium-dependent peptidylarginine deiminase, converting protein arginine residues to citrulline. Its N-terminal cupredoxin-like domain is distinct from its C-terminal catalytic domain, and neither copper binding nor electron transfer follows from the N-terminal classification.

For an uncharacterized member, copper-binding inference needs an appropriate ligand constellation and geometry, supported by metal-occupancy, binding, or spectroscopic evidence. Electron-transfer assignment additionally needs redox and biochemical evidence, ideally identifying physiological partners. Even sequence homology alone does not guarantee retention of these functions. Structural templates and representatives should therefore match the relevant family, domain boundaries, and local functional features; a single cupredoxin-superfamily representative cannot safely stand in for all three biological roles.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
